title One ident rule, twenty-nine copies
prompt The baton, and my own last recommend line naming the next fall the ceiling meter could take.
rota lap 5201, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels. The reading it surfaced is that a number can feel green while being walkable-around: glow_ceiling_refusal fell 40 to 34 on this lap without one site becoming covered, because the six sites left the denominator rather than joining the numerator.
think My last recommend named error.Overflow as the next fall. Measuring rather than trusting it, error.Overflow appears zero times in the uncovered list -- the scan's own string-stripping rule already removes every one as a false pair. The real remainder is error.BadIdent at 35 of 40 uncovered sites, across five ceilings, spread over the lowering room.
think A recommend line written from memory is a forecast, and this one was wrong in the honest direction: it named a real shared name and the wrong one. Running the list took ten seconds and the correction cost nothing, which is the argument for reading the meter rather than the note about the meter.
think Reading the 35 sites showed they are not 35 decisions. fn zig_safe_ident stands in 29 glow/lower_*.rye modules. Strip the max_ constant each names and five distinct bodies remain; 17 are byte-identical. The character test -- first byte a letter or underscore, every later byte a letter, digit or underscore, hyphen mapped to underscore -- is byte-for-byte the same in all 29.
think So the refusal record was not the crux. A record cannot land once while its function is copied 29 times, and threading a slot through 29 copies would be doing the wrong work 29 times. The crux is the duplication, and fixing it makes the record a one-file change.
think What varies between the copies is the bill for writing one rule 29 times, and every item is a disagreement rather than a variation: 23 return usize where TAME asks u32; five carry the invariant assert the other 24 omit; glow/lower_shop_nest.rye answers a LENGTH ceiling with error.MissingFace where 28 peers answer error.BadIdent; glow/lower_alias.rye maps the dot as well as the hyphen.
think The dot is the one real design question and both readings are kept rather than reconciled, because both are right: a face refuses the dot, since a dot is not a Zig identifier byte; a wing lowers it, since records.cur is a path and the underscore is its honest lowering. Named as an enum rather than passed as a bool, so a call site says which it means.
obs Three faults arrive as the one word error.BadIdent -- an empty face, a face past its ceiling, and a byte Zig will not take -- so each fills its own glow/refusal.rye record. The published error name is unmoved, which is the arc's standing rule: a published error set is a contract and the record is what tells its cases apart.
obs Two Reason members were earned rather than assumed: ident_empty at 11 bytes and ident_char_not_allowed at 22, both under the 27 the widest reason still holds, so max_refusal_line_bytes is unmoved and the derivation comment records the re-read rather than a new number.
obs The lap's real finding is about the meter rather than the code, and it came out of watching the fall. glow_ceiling_refusal reads a ceiling site as a comparison against a named max_ CONSTANT. The one rule takes its ceiling as a PARAMETER, so the six converted sites left the denominator: uncovered fell 40 to 34 while recorded held at 8. The record does exist and five witness legs prove it -- and that meter cannot see it, and cannot tell that state from a lap that parameterized a ceiling and wrote nothing at all.
obs So the number is lowerable by a change that covers nothing. That is not a sharper-reading problem, it is a wants-a-sibling problem: glow_ident counts whether the rule is in one place, under its own falling ceiling, and the two meters together cannot both be satisfied by either evasion. Written into the sibling's ceiling arc where a reader of that number will meet it, rather than only here.
obs Booked as a ratchet rather than a red, and the reason is the ledger's own words: a red is something wrong, a ratchet is something not yet uniform. Nothing here answers a question falsely; a site-counting meter lowering when a site leaves is inherent to site counting, and the cure shipped on the same lap as the finding. The pin also stood at 64,638 of 65,536 with a mean row at 2,355, so booking would have required a fold, and folding to record a ratchet is the wrong trade.
obs The cohort was chosen to be provable rather than large. Six modules with six guards of their own, all GREEN after conversion, with every call site keeping its signature -- the stub keeps the local name, the local error and the local return width, so the 17 call sites across the six did not move. The remaining 23 stand under a ceiling that only falls.
obs The control edited the tracked scan in place to prove its ceiling from both sides, which would have left CEILING=1 in the repository had a leg died mid-run. It copies the scan into the pen before the first leg now and never writes the tracked file. Caught by reading the control rather than by a guard, and worth saying plainly: a pen that reaches outside itself is a pen only while every leg passes.
loom witness=refusal_witness legs=21 legs_before=16 failures=0 ident_legs=5
